  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,10).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,10)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(10,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(10) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(10))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
